favorite customers, fix.com.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.870
So the goal for the
session is to help

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.566
you find cats in videos.

00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:34.190
That's really all we're going to
talk about video intelligence.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.027
How can you find your next cat
video as easily as possible?

00:00:37.027 --> 00:00:38.610
Because as you guys
know, the internet

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.449
is all about cat videos.

00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:43.490
And so, the thing that
we're trying to talk about

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.920
is, jokes apart, the
goal for this is really

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.330
think about how can we
make sense of dark content,

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.550
as [INAUDIBLE] put it.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.130
How do you find a favorite,
your relevant entities,

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.480
and video content?

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.644
How can we help you
find potentially,

00:00:55.644 --> 00:00:57.560
if you're thinking about
a media organization.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.780
Like an example is how Google
Play can automatically detect

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.530
appropriate actors, actresses
in videos, et cetera.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.100
How can we help you do
those kind of scenarios?

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.180
Video content it's
exploding every day, right?

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.560
I think it was an
article last week

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.930
talking about how every
day people watch over

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.810
a billion hours of video
content on YouTube.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:20.830
Every day.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.010
And can you imagine-
and there's an article

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.340
in Cisco- Cisco actually had an
article sometime back talking

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.390
about how by 2020, 60% to 70%
of all traffic on the internet

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.010
is going to be video content.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.330
Well, how do you
make sense of that?

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.356
How can you, as an
organization, start

00:01:36.356 --> 00:01:38.730
harnessing all this video
content that's flowing through?

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.980
Sometimes content that
you're publishing,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.970
content that you have access
to, and sometimes it's content

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.160
that you're distributing.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.590
How can you make sense
of this media content,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.120
so you can get metadata and
do things with that content.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.010
Clearly, people have
been doing things

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.980
with structured data
for a long time, right?

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.300
You know VI and data warehousing
has been in the marketplace,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.790
people know what to do with
VI and data warehousing.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.500
How do I think
about querying data?

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.907
How do I find relevant entities?

00:02:05.907 --> 00:02:07.740
How do I walk through
a lot of these pieces?

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.190
Largest aspect of something
that people do already

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.330
in structured data.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.372
But for the most part,
video content as being

00:02:15.372 --> 00:02:16.260
something dark.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.020
People have not
really played with it,

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.219
outside of consuming it.

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:20.760
And really, the goal
for this session

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.218
is how do we enable
you to do that.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.120
For example, how do you
find your favorite dog

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:29.560
in a consumer video?

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.680
Or how do you find a
video of a sports event

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.480
across your petabytes
of video content

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:35.850
if you're a media organization?

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.490
How can we enable
you to do that?

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.281
And that's really what
we're going to spend

00:02:39.281 --> 00:02:40.469
some time talking about.

00:02:40.469 --> 00:02:42.760
Another aspect that you guys
can imagine more and more,

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.570
is more and more video content
and not actually professionally

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:49.840
created content, but it's
actually crowd created content.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.770
Same way as images have
exploded on mobile cameras,

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:57.410
people are now creating more and
more videos on mobile devices.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.070
So we want to start
understanding this content

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.104
from these devices.

00:03:01.104 --> 00:03:03.270
And so you can start driving
better recommendations,

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.500
better [? hat ?] targeting,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.166
And that's really the
goal is, how do we

00:03:07.166 --> 00:03:09.440
do that with video content?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.040
So Google provides a
pretty broad platform

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.310
for machine learning.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.092
We'll spend a couple
of cycles on this

00:03:15.092 --> 00:03:16.550
so you guys get
the context of what

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.080
are we talking about today.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.965
So as an organization,
one aspect

00:03:20.965 --> 00:03:23.890
is we provide you a
platform that you can build

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.270
your data to Google Cloud.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:27.760
You have a set of
data scientists

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.050
in your organization.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.330
They can then build their
own machine learning module

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.430
using TensorFlow,
and then we provide

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.180
you a managed service runtime
using our cloud ML engine.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:40.270
So the case on
the left is really

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:44.350
your data, your model graph,
running on cloud ML using

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:45.696
an open source framework.

00:03:45.696 --> 00:03:47.320
The one on the right
is really thinking

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:50.995
about how we can use some of
our own Google internal models-

00:03:50.995 --> 00:03:53.036
like our apply the [? rate ?]
targeted scenarios,

00:03:53.036 --> 00:03:55.120
and in how you can use
that to harness that.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.980
So it's really targeted
more app developers.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.100
So basically in the
case for vision,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.080
we've used models
that power things

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.480
like image search, models
that power things like

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.320
Google Photos.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.840
We've exposed that as an API.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.640
And so basically
you consume that

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.710
with minimal customization.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.420
And so the new
[INAUDIBLE] API we

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.160
want to spend some
time talking about

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.470
is our Video Intelligence API.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.140
So Video Intelligence
API is all about how

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.980
you can help use Google models
that power all first party

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.070
Google products like
YouTube, Google Photos.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.350
And how you can harness
the same technology

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:33.170
for your own unique scenario.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.240
So what are the key
use cases that we're

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.340
trying to solve for
Video Intelligence?

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.470
Really we're thinking
about three main things.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.060
How do you find the main topics
of what the video is about?

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.810
So for example, what
is a video about?

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:47.330
What are the key relevant
entities within the video?

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.830
So those are really
what-- just kind

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.510
of thing of the
fundamental use case

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.260
that we're trying
to solve for first.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.040
The second big use case is, if
anything vision has taught me,

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.230
is many organizations
really want

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.910
to monitor
inappropriate content.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.325
Especially if you think about
your crowd-sourced platform

00:05:01.325 --> 00:05:03.200
and there's a lot of
people uploading content

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.220
into that device.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.930
How do you monitor and manage
what inappropriate content

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:07.870
that flows through?

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.750
And that's a key
use case that people

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.960
have to deal with, especially
in Cloud [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:12.920
crowd-sourced content.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.480
And the third part
is how do we find

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.340
the most relevant
moments in a video

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.200
so you can start publishing it?

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.570
Unlike a document,
unlike an image,

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.920
if you have a 10
minute long video,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.382
how do you entice somebody
to watch that video, right?

00:05:25.382 --> 00:05:26.840
So really thinking
about highlights

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.480
and thinking about
video previews.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.574
It's a pretty key problem
in the video use case.

00:05:30.574 --> 00:05:32.990
And that's another scenario
we'll talk a little bit about.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:34.491
So really key, the
three part things

00:05:34.491 --> 00:05:35.906
we are going to
talk about, is how

00:05:35.906 --> 00:05:37.730
do you find the right
entity in the video

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.472
or what the video
is about, how do

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:40.970
we moderate
inappropriate content,

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:43.130
and how you think
about finding the best

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.900
moments within the video.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.279
So let's start off
with the first aspect,

00:05:48.279 --> 00:05:49.320
how do you find entities?

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.560
So really we have a feature
in the Video Intelligence

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.550
API called label detection.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:57.690
So label detection
is basically--

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.280
if you have label
detection on vision,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.260
this applies
primarily on images.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.970
I mean, now we have label
detection on video content.

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.750
So really its goal is to
find thousands and thousands

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.650
of different entities
in video content.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.490
These entities can be
things like everyday objects

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.590
from chairs, to
things, to activities

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.600
like running, jumping, falling.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.310
Or even things like
scenarios like how

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.287
do you think about
everyday objects like cars,

00:06:20.287 --> 00:06:21.370
transportation, et cetera.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:24.300
And we'll talk a little bit
about those entities later.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.040
And how do you call this API?

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.960
So really the thing is
you're passing the URI, which

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.980
is basically a GCS location, a
Google Cloud Storage location

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.350
for where the video is.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:34.955
And then you can
also tell it where

00:06:34.955 --> 00:06:36.413
do you want to
write the output to.

00:06:36.413 --> 00:06:38.117
So it's really--
the one caveat I

00:06:38.117 --> 00:06:40.200
want to make sure you guys
leave with, in V1 we're

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.540
really focused on
back scenarios and not

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.160
real time streaming scenarios.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.950
So really it's whereas you
have a piece of video content

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.140
on Google Cloud Storage
that we read from

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.520
and then we write back to.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.470
So there are lots of
other streaming scenarios

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.170
that you can imagine that we're
not solving for that in V1,

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.500
still it's something we're
thinking about down the line

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.892
for future versions.

00:06:59.892 --> 00:07:02.100
And the other part that we
want to spend some time on

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.092
is I'll talk a
little bit about is

00:07:04.092 --> 00:07:05.550
what do you want
to see in a video,

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:06.780
and when do you want
to see the video.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.400
So we give you a
couple of aspects.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.400
We give you things like video
level annotation that tells you

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.150
this video is about blah.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:13.650
Right?

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.300
So what is this video about?

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.760
And then the second part
is when in the video

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:18.850
did something occur?

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.200
And for that there are
different ways you can do that.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.700
There are things called-- what
we have called shot mode that

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.197
tells you tell me things
relevant to the shot,

00:07:26.197 --> 00:07:28.530
and the things that are
relevant at a frame level, which

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.050
is basically just applying
Vision API on a frame

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.180
by frame perspective.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.554
And we'll talk a little bit
about the differences in when

00:07:34.554 --> 00:07:39.820
you would use A versus B.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:41.280
So the response
that you get back,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.610
and I'm going to spend
some time on this.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.443
Really, the thing that
you get back from us,

00:07:44.443 --> 00:07:47.590
it will tell you the entity,
so what the entity is about.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:50.560
Right now all the entities
are written in English.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.292
Our goal over time is to
support additional languages.

00:07:53.292 --> 00:07:55.750
And then we tell you where in
the video something occurred.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.090
So we tell you first the level,
whether it's a video level

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:58.590
entity.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.000
Meaning this entity
really defines

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.180
what the video is about,
or whether this video

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.590
occurs at a certain shot.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.040
In which case we tell you
the start and end time,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.185
or just we tell you--

00:08:09.185 --> 00:08:10.810
if you want to do a
frame level entity,

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.694
we'll tell you it occurs
at a certain frame.

00:08:12.694 --> 00:08:14.235
And one of the things
I want to spend

00:08:14.235 --> 00:08:15.985
some time more on over
the next few slides

00:08:15.985 --> 00:08:18.010
is why do we give
you so many options.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:19.536
Because understanding
video content

00:08:19.536 --> 00:08:21.160
can be a little
complicated, and that's

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.618
the reason why we give you all
these different permutations

00:08:23.618 --> 00:08:25.480
of content coming back at you.

00:08:28.284 --> 00:08:30.200
So the first thing we
talk about is how do you

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.130
separate signal from noise.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.299
That's one of the
reasons why we do this.

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.490
Because the challenge
with video content

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.770
is if you run an
hour long video with,

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.131
let's say, a shot
changes every minute.

00:08:40.131 --> 00:08:41.630
That's meaning that
the camera angle

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.010
goes from looking like
this or looking like this.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.390
So can you imagine
that's a shot change,

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:48.140
the entire camera changes.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.490
Those kinds of scenarios, if you
think about an hour long video,

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:54.200
which changes every
hour, every minute,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.450
so that's roughly
60 shots, right?

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:00.310
The question is, what do you
want to understand out of that?

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.410
So it depends on
level of granularity,

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:03.830
depending on use
case-- you guys,

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:05.970
people think of
different things.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:09.240
So the first thing we do is we
give you a video level entity.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:10.250
So what is it good for?

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.440
So it's really
focused around this

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:16.280
is kind of truly where we
talk about more than just

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.250
a frame level annotation.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:21.375
Meaning that we just don't use
Vision API to look at something

00:09:21.375 --> 00:09:22.250
and give you results.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.770
We're actually doing additional
machine learning logic

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.450
on top of each frame.

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:28.820
And Juhyun is going to
spend some time about that.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:31.880
But one of the
caveats is this really

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:35.810
applies only when the entire
video is about a certain thing.

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.630
And it's really
optimized for scenarios

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:42.140
where it's shot from content
minimally one to five minutes.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.070
So if you give us
a hour long TV show

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:48.244
and you say, hey, give me
this video level entity,

00:09:48.244 --> 00:09:50.660
it's going to give you a very
high level generic entities.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:51.430
And that's something
you guys should

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.820
be aware of, because
video level annotations

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:58.150
it really makes sense more
for shot from content.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.210
The other aspect is
shot level annotations.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:03.640
So think of a shot
as a mini video,

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:05.890
meaning that's kind
of grouped together

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:09.027
based on the shot change and
so almost as a mini video.

00:10:09.027 --> 00:10:11.110
And this is kind of a first
level temporal entity.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.045
So will tell you
when in the video

00:10:13.045 --> 00:10:15.670
something occurred and will kind
of group it at the shot level.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:18.070
So it will tell you just start
and end time of the shot,

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.930
and within that
shot we'll tell you

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.870
these are the most relevant
entities in that shot.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:26.940
So where does that
kind of come into play?

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.910
You can imagine especially
when you have like,

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.227
let's say, you have
an hour long TV show.

00:10:31.227 --> 00:10:32.060
You really want to--

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.670
there's going to be a lot of
context switching in that TV

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:35.171
show, right?

00:10:35.171 --> 00:10:37.711
People are going to be-- some
people are going to be suddenly

00:10:37.711 --> 00:10:40.550
talking inside, people are
going to be going outside.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.764
Each shot has a different
set of relevant entities

00:10:43.764 --> 00:10:45.680
and what you really care
about tell me exactly

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:46.931
what's relevant within a shot.

00:10:46.931 --> 00:10:48.430
Because otherwise
you're going to be

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.270
dealing with a lot of
noise coming at you.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:53.300
Because, for example, we'll
talk a little bit about an event

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.970
later where, for example-- we
have it in a video-- but say

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.620
I am talking right now.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.787
And if somebody does a
frame level annotation

00:10:59.787 --> 00:11:02.120
they literally are going to
get the same set of entities

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:03.710
every frame every second.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:06.420
That's a lot of noise
coming back at you, right?

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.480
So that's really
where shots really

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.850
help you there because it's
going to basically aggregate

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:11.930
that information
and say, hey, guess

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.240
what this is what's really
relevant within this video.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.370
So you're not dealing with all
that noise coming back at you.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:18.770
That's why it kind of goes
back to separating signal

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:19.800
from noise.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.060
And the other value is
that over time we also

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:24.710
can start adding
additional signals.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.810
So you can start adding more
than just visual signals,

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:28.730
additional signals can
flow in and tell you

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:29.840
what the shot is about.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.829
And Juhyun will cover a
little bit about that later.

00:11:32.829 --> 00:11:34.620
So this is where we
can-- so this is really

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.200
thinking about things
about context switching.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.420
So think about new
shows, TV shows.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.900
Think about aspects,
like for example,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:47.220
even like basically the video
we showed at the keynote demo

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.450
Sarah did.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.300
Like an ad as multiple
different context switching.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.100
A shot based entity would be
very relevant in that scenario.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.290
And finally we have the
lowest level of granularity,

00:11:58.290 --> 00:11:59.210
which is frame level.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:01.670
So frame level is really
thinking about there's

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:03.060
no additional ML logic.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.420
It's really just banging,
taking every frame at one FPF.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.310
And you're banging
against a image detector

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:11.624
and saying, hey what do you
see in this frame of the video.

00:12:11.624 --> 00:12:14.040
So there's a little less logic,
a little less intelligence

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.280
in this aspect, but there
are a lot of scenarios

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:17.640
where this makes sense.

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:20.220
For example, there's a lot of
scenarios in, say, surveillance

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:21.740
where the video is
not moving, it's

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:23.610
always pointing in
a certain direction.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:26.490
And which case if
I do a shot change,

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:29.310
there is no shot change there's
just always one shot, right?

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:33.072
So at that base, frame level
entities make a lot more sense.

00:12:33.072 --> 00:12:34.530
The other thing is
consumer videos.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:38.444
For example, if it's a long
form video where you really

00:12:38.444 --> 00:12:39.860
want to get a
different-- you want

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:41.540
to understand exactly
what's happening

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:44.530
at a much more granular level
than even at the shot level.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:46.590
Some of these frame level
entities make sense.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.010
So this is cloud.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.530
And I kept reading
a lot of tweets

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:57.030
yesterday saying there was not
enough demos of the keynotes.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:59.630
So the goal today is to
show you guys some demos.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:04.970
So if we switch to
my demo machine,

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:08.410
and if I can actually log in.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:09.480
OK.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.510
So by the way the whole
goal for this session

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:13.100
is how do I get
back to BigQuery.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.320
Because I love BigQuery
so my goal is in the end

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:17.510
I'm going to end up
back in BigQuery.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.070
So let's first go
with this video.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:21.890
So in this case this
is a video about--

00:13:27.524 --> 00:13:28.940
I'm sorry, let me
just refresh it.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:38.540
This is not a good sign.

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:44.727
