00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:50.340
OK.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:53.890
While that's coming up, so for
example, let's go to this one.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:59.656
So this case, this is an ad for
one of our Google Firewalls.

00:13:59.656 --> 00:14:02.030
We're basically just pulling
ads from our Google channel.

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:04.680
As you can imagine, videos
have a much higher rate

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.270
of copyright
infringement, et cetera.

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:09.010
So we're primarily using
our own Google content.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:11.850
So in this case, we're
just showing you an ad of--

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:15.317
this is an example where context
is switching a lot, right?

00:14:15.317 --> 00:14:17.400
It's an ad, so you can
imagine every three or four

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.980
seconds you switch context
pretty significantly.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.740
And we basically-- the
key aspect is we're

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.420
showing you how this changes.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:28.050
So for example, what you
can do is basically draw

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:31.450
a list of all the entities
that are in this video.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:33.150
So, for example,
in this case this

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:36.870
is primarily our entire JSON
dump of every single entity

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:38.640
that we detected.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:42.090
We tell you the shot level,
video level about this entity.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.880
The one gap we have
here is, for example,

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.320
because it's changing
context so much,

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:50.699
our video level entities
don't add a lot of value.

00:14:50.699 --> 00:14:51.490
Does it make sense?

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:52.740
Because it's such a--

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.240
you can imagine which
things from outside

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.180
to inside and a lot of
things changing around.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:01.122
Over time you can imagine as
we improve our video models

00:15:01.122 --> 00:15:02.830
and we start taking
in additional signals

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:05.330
through our reader models, we
can pick up additional signals

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.410
to make our reader
classifier more smart.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.230
And that's something we
are constantly working on.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:13.120
So in this case, for
example, we find a car.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:17.300
We tell you exact-- there's a
shot boundary where the car is.

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:19.030
And the other aspect
is, for example, we

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:21.020
see homes, a lot of
different pieces that's

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:22.262
flying around here.

00:15:22.262 --> 00:15:23.720
The other thing
is, for example, we

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:25.594
know in that aspect
we're actually in the car

00:15:25.594 --> 00:15:27.207
because the scene
is changing so fast.

00:15:27.207 --> 00:15:28.790
So the video will
actually think we're

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.030
in a car and kind of
driving, that scenario.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:35.030
So [INAUDIBLE] so you can
imagine common scenarios.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:37.280
So our use case is
where we drive that.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:39.950
So let me see if I can
come back to this case.

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:42.290
So in this case, this
is a video about what's

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.300
going on inside a zoo, right?

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.750
The entire video is actually
happening inside the zoo.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:49.250
It's a nice friendly
video about animals.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:51.110
So in this case our
video level entities

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:53.660
actually makes a lot of sense
because we're not changing

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:54.980
context for the entire time.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.840
Our context is a
homogeneous video

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:00.050
about a tour of a
potential wildlife thing.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:03.620
So we can actually find out
it's about a zoo, it's wildlife,

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:06.410
it's tourism, it can give you
much more relevant video level

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:09.160
entities in this case.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.270
But we also can do things
like shot level entities.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.420
So in this case, we can
look at these pieces,

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.330
and you can imagine each
of the slices are shots.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:20.780
Again, this is an ad, so
the shots in this video

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:22.517
are changing constantly, right?

00:16:22.517 --> 00:16:24.350
You're going to go every
five/six seconds is

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.900
changing a lot on you.

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.240
And so you can imagine as
we go from shot to shot

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.435
we start telling you all the
different pieces that flow in.

00:16:32.435 --> 00:16:34.310
And a couple of things
that's relevant if you

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:36.268
think about digital
marketing scenarios, right?

00:16:36.268 --> 00:16:38.665
It's a zoom video,
but there are a lot

00:16:38.665 --> 00:16:40.790
of things about mobile
phones and devices in there.

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:43.535
So if you really kind of
mine the data to see what

00:16:43.535 --> 00:16:45.020
are all the entities in there.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:46.910
This is a great way to kind of
capture all that information.

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:48.451
Because you would
never have thought,

00:16:48.451 --> 00:16:50.767
why would I see a mobile
device in my zoo video?

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:59.214
So here is another
aspect of a long video.

00:16:59.214 --> 00:17:00.880
For example, in this
case, it's actually

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:04.599
a video of [INAUDIBLE] giving
a speech at a conference.

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:07.630
And this is an example for
where the number of shots--

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:09.910
and this is actually
not that many, right?

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:11.560
There's a lot of
shots, for example,

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.860
in this case she's
actually standing in one

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:15.109
place for pretty much for all.

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:17.844
She's just-- the video
follows her for a full minute.

00:17:17.844 --> 00:17:20.260
And so you imagine there's
really no shot changes in that.

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.119
So it's actually just
about her talking.

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:24.119
So you can imagine
where a frame level

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.910
annotation in this context
would be extremely noisy,

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:28.660
because you're just
going to get bombarded

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:31.000
with 60 frame annotations
that tells you

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:31.980
exactly the same thing.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:33.470
It's not really
relevant anymore.

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:35.386
And that's why shot level
aggregation is going

00:17:35.386 --> 00:17:36.980
to be useful in that aspect.

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:38.480
And that's also a
unique proposition

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.040
from the video
perspective, we think,

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.560
compared to some of our
competitors in the marketplace

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.610
today.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:45.510
So that's one aspect.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.259
So what can you do
with all this metadata?

00:17:47.259 --> 00:17:49.654
And as I said, my goal
is always to come back

00:17:49.654 --> 00:17:51.320
to saying if you go
back to the scenario

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:53.640
on how we talked
about structured data,

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:56.010
how do we enable these things
in really structured data?

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:58.890
All of this is just JSON
that's flowing through, right?

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:02.760
So you can imagine we can start
basically making this JSON

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.070
and flatten this JSON.

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:07.740
It's as simple-- you can take as
JSON out, flatten it, and then

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:09.580
load it into BigQuery.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:12.420
And now that it's in
BigQuery I can do things.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:14.040
I can just write a
regular SQL query

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:15.720
like I would do everything else.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.990
So now I want to understand,
for example, in this SQL

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.070
query all I'm trying to
do is, hey, tell me off

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.760
of all the shots
what's the most common.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:25.470
By scene, what's the
most relevant entity

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:26.410
in all my shots.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:28.240
So I can do a simple
SQL query, come back,

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:29.370
get that information out.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:30.870
So really what I'm
trying to do here

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.460
is I've basically taken
all this video metadata,

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:37.350
taken all that, plugged
it, did the video API,

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:40.110
and brought it back into
BigQuery and run SQL queries.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:43.980
As I said, the world
revolves around SQL.

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:44.890
Nobody got that joke.

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:46.680
So I've been in SQL for a while.

00:18:53.428 --> 00:18:55.250
Here is another example of that.

00:19:00.304 --> 00:19:01.720
So this is kind
of things that you

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:03.929
could imagine you can easily
do with the video intel.

00:19:03.929 --> 00:19:05.928
That's really the value
that we're trying to do,

00:19:05.928 --> 00:19:08.590
is we're trying to start making
sense of all this metadata

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:10.048
that you have in
your organization.

00:19:10.048 --> 00:19:12.730
If you think about it, you now
have petabytes and petabytes

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:13.650
of media content.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.400
You can run it through
Video Intelligence,

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:19.079
start harnessing those entities,
flow it into a database,

00:19:19.079 --> 00:19:20.620
and start to actually
think about how

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.480
you can now index
your videos more

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:25.660
intelligently, feed it to
your media portals, fitted

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:27.996
to your query
engines, do analytics.

00:19:27.996 --> 00:19:29.620
Think about scenarios
where you can now

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:33.970
correlate your entities in the
video to potentially ratings.

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:36.890
So you can figure out when
certain shows are going bad

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:38.600
based on ratings,
or can find which

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:40.190
entity were people looking at.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.190
You can start doing more
interesting scenarios.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.539
Now that you have
this as metadata

00:19:44.539 --> 00:19:46.330
you can do a lot more
interesting scenarios

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:48.970
on the correlation, driving
the downstream, mission-only

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:51.628
models, et cetera.

00:19:51.628 --> 00:19:53.044
Back to slides.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.574
So with that, I want to ask
Juhyun to come on stage.

00:19:58.574 --> 00:20:00.490
Juhyun's going to talk
a little bit about some

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:03.024
of the underlying technology
that we're doing, and also

00:20:03.024 --> 00:20:04.440
talking about where
we're thinking

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:06.766
of taking it down the way, too.

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:25.090
JUHYUN LEE: All right.

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:27.344
Hi.

00:20:27.344 --> 00:20:29.023
Let's go back.

00:20:29.023 --> 00:20:34.140
So hi, I'm Juhyun from the
Video Intelligence team.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:37.600
I'm a software engineer
in motion perception,

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.890
which is an organization
under Google research.

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:43.820
And our mission is to
make machines understand

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:49.380
unstructured data such as video,
audio, images, and et cetera.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:53.930
I'm here to explain what's
going on under the hood

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:56.520
so that it doesn't look
like the wall of Google

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.462
is selling black
magic to consumers.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:04.680
So given a video
in your GCS bucket,

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.740
the first thing that we
do is we decode the video.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.780
And usually a video
container contains

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:12.020
like audio stream,
video stream, subtitles,

00:21:12.020 --> 00:21:14.100
and all of the metadata.

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:17.055
And we are first interested
in the video stream.

00:21:17.055 --> 00:21:20.940
And a video stream
is often a sequence

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:23.880
of images, a lot of them.

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:26.960
For a five-minute video at 24
frames per second you will have

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:30.140
like 7,000/8,000 images in them.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:34.230
And they will probably in
sequence look a lot similar.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:40.380
So classifying each of those is
not too efficient, let's say.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:43.600
So we shot sample the
images, and say at one FPS.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:49.520
So one frame per second we
apply an image classifier,

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:51.420
an image classifier
that is very similar

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:55.680
that you can get through
the Google Cloud Vision API.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:59.720
And we do it for every
image in the entire video.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:02.850
And for each frame, you
will get image features

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:08.070
such as this elephant with
97% accuracy, or animal,

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:09.834
or river in that case.

00:22:09.834 --> 00:22:11.250
And if you keep
going on, you will

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:15.870
get like other labels
for crocodiles and lions.

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:17.700
And at the end of
the video what we do

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.360
is we have an aggregating
video classifier,

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:23.760
and this is really
what sits at the core

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:28.480
of this particular API.

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:32.310
And given all these features,
throughout the entire video

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:34.710
it will then say, A,
this is a documentary

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:35.730
about animals in Africa.

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:40.680
Another similar
example would be if you

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:46.290
have a video of people
in costumes or pumpkins

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:51.060
and candies, this classifier
will be able to tell you,

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:53.430
hey, this is a video
about Halloween.

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:55.530
And this is only
possible because we

00:22:55.530 --> 00:23:00.710
have trained this model on
millions of YouTube videos,

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:03.265
and human rated and
also like verified.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:14.130
Ram also talked about also the
shop level annotation a minute

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:14.880
ago.

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:16.920
And those are essentially
treating each shot

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:22.250
as a mini video, and then
running with the same logic.

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:24.750
Of course, there's more machine
learning and computer vision

00:23:24.750 --> 00:23:27.780
techniques involved
in this, I am just

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:29.700
very briefly summarize this.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:33.060
But you are essentially
getting what's behind YouTube

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:35.040
and photos with this release.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.520
So far I've only talked
about the visual features,

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:43.660
but we are also considering
adding audio or motion

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:46.600
or speech or subtitles
to this classifier,

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:50.590
and do a final fusion in
this aggregating classifier.

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:55.630
So that you don't assume that
you have a video of an animated

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:56.510
music video.

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:59.620
It will be sad if we
give you a label saying

00:23:59.620 --> 00:24:01.742
that it's an animated cartoon.

00:24:01.742 --> 00:24:03.700
You definitely want your
audio signal in there.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:05.650
So we are looking at
various audio signals

00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:07.730
to improve the
quality of the model.

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:12.220
And one thing that I wanted to
emphasize is with this release,

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:15.250
we are not keeping
the models as is.

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:20.560
We are constantly improving the
model, especially when Google

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:25.660
internally releases a new
model for the YouTube photos,

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:31.090
then we also update the models
for Google Cloud video API.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:36.960
And also your
feedback is important.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.290
Your feedback may make
me happy or not so happy.

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:42.380
Those are incorporated
in the next releases.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:47.905
So yeah, feel free to
reach out to us to improve.

00:24:47.905 --> 00:24:48.560
All right.

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:57.720
Yeah, that's about leaking the
secret recipe that we have.

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:01.280
Now I'm going to talk a
little bit about what's

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:05.330
coming next, a preview
on video previews,

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:08.900
also known as a video
summaries or thumbnailer.

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:16.670
So assume that you have a
bunch of customer videos

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:18.560
and you have to
show it in a list,

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:21.250
you need to make a listing page.

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:24.680
Now you need thumbnails
for each of those videos.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:28.160
How do you pick the right
video frame for the video

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.690
that you don't know about?

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:33.030
You could pick the first
frame in the video,

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:36.860
or you could apply the
Wadsworth Constant,

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:38.720
and skip to 30 second--

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:42.110
30% HD video and
then show that frame.

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:44.850
At Google we use
machine learning.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:48.920
And what we do is we have a
machine learned classifier that

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:53.780
tells what's interesting-ness
of this particular video

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:57.620
frame as a thumbnail.

00:25:57.620 --> 00:26:01.790
So we ran it through a
lot of YouTube videos,

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:04.910
human rated and also
verified, these are actually

00:26:04.910 --> 00:26:08.060
positive with AB testing.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:11.330
And, yeah, so here
on the screen you

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:16.085
can see videos, the link to
the YouTube video actually.

00:26:16.085 --> 00:26:19.950
And in the middle column
you see a static thumbnail.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:24.790
So the single frame that has the
highest interesting-ness score.

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:28.190
And on the rightmost column
you see moving thumbnails.

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:32.930
What's the most interesting
three second clip of that video

00:26:32.930 --> 00:26:37.130
based on the machine classifier?

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:43.850
For example, let's show
earth view in Google Maps.

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:50.420
So this is the video when Google
Maps released the earth view,

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:55.340
and that's the corresponding
static thumbnail

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:56.630
and the dynamic thumbnail.

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:03.470
So this is when we
announced the Cloud Vision

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:08.180
API in December of 2015.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:13.790
The whole video is about
presenting what Cloud Vision

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:20.270
API can do, and a demo on this
small little Raspberry robot.

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:24.410
So it correctly picks up
on the interesting thing,

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:30.080
the only thing in the video,
or like the moving thumbnail.

00:27:30.080 --> 00:27:32.750
One thing that
you may ask is why

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:36.440
is the static thumbnail not a
subset of the actual animation

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:37.700
on the right.

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:40.234
And that's because the
two classifiers are doing

00:27:40.234 --> 00:27:41.400
completely different things.

00:27:41.400 --> 00:27:46.260
For example the right-most
column also incorporates motion

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:51.230
in deciding should this be
the moving thumbnail or not,

00:27:51.230 --> 00:27:54.830
therefore you cannot do
that in a static thumbnail.

00:27:54.830 --> 00:28:04.520
So with that, let me hand it
back to Ram for more use cases.

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:06.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:10.746
RAM RAMANATHAN: If
you just go back

00:28:10.746 --> 00:28:11.870
to one second on the right.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:13.910
I think a couple of
things that I really

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:17.360
loved about this demo is that
this Cloud Vision API was

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:18.980
really all about the robot.

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:21.430
And the fact that it picked
up that the key thing about it

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:24.140
is actually about the robot,
the Raspberry Pi [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:26.547
robot that shows up, is
actually pretty unique.

00:28:26.547 --> 00:28:28.130
Because it kind of
somehow figured out

00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:31.040
that this video is
really about how

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.089
we think about Vision
API applying the robot

00:28:33.089 --> 00:28:34.130
on a little Raspberry Pi.

00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:36.140
And it figured out that that's
the most relevant thumbnails

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:37.030
to pick up.

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:38.700
It's actually, I mean, if you
think about this problem--

00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:39.950
if you go back to the slides--

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:42.960
if you think about this problem
for a organization that's

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:44.630
building media
portals and people

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.460
are dealing with millions
of video content.

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:50.150
I mean, if you have petabytes of
content in your media archive,

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:51.270
how do you consume it?

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:52.520
And that's really what
we're thinking about,

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.006
how do we make life easier?

00:28:54.006 --> 00:28:56.630
So the two use case I'm going to
spend some time talking about.

00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:59.760
First is thinking about
content recommendation.

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:01.980
So what I mean by that?

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:06.020
So for example, you think of
us, think of this as a mini

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:07.120
YouTube, right?

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:10.040
It's really not YouTube in
a specific infrastructure,

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:11.180
but think of a YouTube.

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:14.570
Basically, you've got a set of
videos on Google Cloud Storage,

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:17.460
you want to start deriving
some metadata on this video.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:21.140
It then flows into potentially
BigQuery to do some analysis,

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:22.780
and then does a bunch
of data analysts

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.230
to look at this content,
do some queries on that.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:26.960
And then the drive
creates some features,

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:30.340
they feed it into a cloud ML
model to drive recommendations.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.090
At the same time they're
collecting things

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:34.414
like fixed stream behavior
to drive the scenario,

00:29:34.414 --> 00:29:36.080
and it kind of flows
through that thing.

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:38.870
So that's really thinking about
a very common recommendation

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:41.350
scenario.

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:42.770
Do people actually do that?

00:29:42.770 --> 00:29:44.480
So here is a public
research paper

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:47.030
that YouTube published
some time back.

00:29:47.030 --> 00:29:49.350
So in this case, think of
this video [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.320
This video [INAUDIBLE]
is based on billions

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:53.870
and billions and
billions of videos

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:55.730
that people load into YouTube.

00:29:55.730 --> 00:29:58.220
And you and me as a
user come into YouTube

00:29:58.220 --> 00:29:59.714
to figure out what
to watch, right?

00:29:59.714 --> 00:30:01.130
So the first thing
people actually

00:30:01.130 --> 00:30:03.090
do is something like
collaborative filtering where

00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:04.580
they're really
thinking about, how

00:30:04.580 --> 00:30:08.090
do I apply what's kind
of the set of hundreds

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:11.381
of potentially targets that
we can show to the end user?

00:30:11.381 --> 00:30:13.130
So that's really where
people are thinking

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:15.537
about pretty sophisticated
TensorFlow based models

00:30:15.537 --> 00:30:17.620
to really think about that
collaborative filtering

00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:19.130
to drive that scenario.

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.870
Then the next thing is really
want to think about this,

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:23.000
how do I then do ranking.

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:25.620
So all of these potentially
hundreds of content,

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:28.580
what's the 25 that I show
to the end user right there?

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:31.030
That's where they apply
second cycle downstream search

00:30:31.030 --> 00:30:35.012
models that then sort of do a
kind of scenario on ranking.

00:30:35.012 --> 00:30:36.470
But a key aspect
to keep in mind is

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:39.380
to drive both these machine
learning models they need

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:40.880
to get video features to flow.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:43.250
And they need metadata
on what the video is

00:30:43.250 --> 00:30:45.320
about to do the recommendation.

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:47.030
Because the recommendation
is both user

00:30:47.030 --> 00:30:49.607
behavior and metadata
about the video.

00:30:49.607 --> 00:30:51.440
And how do you get
metadata about the video?

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:53.880
You have to use something
like Video Intelligence.

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:56.960
But that's another aspect.

00:30:56.960 --> 00:30:59.000
The other big scenario
is media portal,

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:01.730
just take another example
from the world of--

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:05.002
thing is I have
millions of content.

00:31:05.002 --> 00:31:06.710
I want to start exposing
content in a lot

00:31:06.710 --> 00:31:08.390
of different interesting ways.

00:31:08.390 --> 00:31:09.351
How do I do that?

00:31:09.351 --> 00:31:11.850
That's where some of the things
that Juhyun just pointed out

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:15.170
on thumbnails, around all
kinds of moving images

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:17.960
really start coming into
play on video summaries.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:20.780
You just can't show a
two by three pixellation

00:31:20.780 --> 00:31:23.570
view of any video and expect
people to click on it.

00:31:23.570 --> 00:31:26.320
What you really want to do start
enticing them to click on that.

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:28.400
How do you entice them
to click on a video?

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:30.950
You have to show what's relevant
in the video as a thumbnail,

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:32.367
because people are
drawn visually.

00:31:32.367 --> 00:31:34.033
They're going to first
look at something

00:31:34.033 --> 00:31:35.330
and then read the captioning.

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:37.100
Very few people are going
to read the captioning first

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:38.460
and then look at an image.

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:41.870
So you want to make sure you're
showing the most relevant image

00:31:41.870 --> 00:31:44.450
of that video, and then you
want an easy way for someone

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:46.640
to get a feel for what
that video is about.

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:48.704
That's why those
things on moving images

00:31:48.704 --> 00:31:50.120
come into play,
because you really

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:54.590
want a way for our end user
to kind of over our over video

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:57.260
and get a really quick glance
of what this video is about.

00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:59.672
And then you draw them
to the actual video.

00:31:59.672 --> 00:32:01.130
That's why these
kind of techniques

00:32:01.130 --> 00:32:03.630
when you think about building
media portals, video previews,

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:06.810
really start coming to play.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:09.310
With that, what I want
to do is [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:12.146
talk about recommendations,
media portals.

00:32:12.146 --> 00:32:13.520
There are lots of
other scenarios

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:14.895
that we want to
enable with this,

00:32:14.895 --> 00:32:18.270
but you think about a lot of
customers thinking about how

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:20.050
they drive media workflows.

00:32:20.050 --> 00:32:22.820
One of our customer
reference is [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:25.070
So they're really thinking
about how to use metadata

00:32:25.070 --> 00:32:26.300
from these videos.

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:28.437
They drive it into their
media workflow engine.

00:32:28.437 --> 00:32:30.770
So they enabled media publishers
and media organizations

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:34.870
to drive media workflows based
on entities that flow in.

